A||N||6||TTiger||Has anyone tried doing this swap....||||5||
Z||000000||TTiger||02-24-2001||12:13 AM||||I was wondering if anyone has ever tried dropping in a newer (1996+) supercharged 3.8L Pontiac V6 in their Fiero.  I am looking into the conversion right now, but I can't find a whole lot of information on it.  If anyone has done the swap, let me know of any problems or concerns you had.  More importantly, if it is even possible.  I'd like to get some opinions before buying an engine.<BR>Email me or leave a post if you have any information.<P>Tony  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||02-24-2001||12:26 AM||||Do a search on 3800SC or 3800.  It's a VERY popular swap, and there are companies that have taken all the guesswork out for you.  Also, it's a Buick engine, not a Pontiac.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||TTiger||02-24-2001||12:30 AM||||Wow, that was fast.  I'll try a search on that.  <BR>I knew that the engine was Buick, but that's not what I typed for some reason <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR>I'm waiting on a response from my parts supplier on an engine right now.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Tony||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||02-24-2001||01:10 AM||||We do 'em before breakfast....||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||02-24-2001||01:16 AM||||I've just started doing this swap.<P>Terryk seems to be the man with all the info on this one.<P>You will find that no 3800SC ever came with a manual, so using a manual brings up a lot of issues re. the PCM, flywheel, trans strength, etc. that I'm not going to deal with on my 1st 3800SC swap.<P> Actually, the 3800SC came in a bunch of GM cars.  Mine is from an 97 Olds LSS, and I brought it complete with the 4T65E 4speed AOD.<P> You will need to modify the cradle & mounts, along with the wiring harness modifications and shifter issues.   <P> Or Chris West at West Coast Fieros has all the stuff you need.<P>  <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <P> Also ACE has a kit for the 3800SC swap.<P>I have not used any kit parts at this time.<P>PJ's Auto, Inc, in NY, is where I found the complete engine/trans/wiring harness/pcm (amoung the other things that were needed)<BR>for under $2,000 and I highly recommend them, as they didn't just torch out the powertrain, but listened to my requirements and delivered on them.  If you want contact info, just email me.<P>Good Luck<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||02-24-2001||10:45 AM||||There are several people that have done the swap (done vs had it done). Do a search of the archives. There are some different details between the 92-95 and 96+ engines (oil filter adapters, etc). <P>There are advantanges and disadvantages to Series I vs II engines depending on what you are planning long term. The Series II engines are very nice, but there's not enough difference to justify the price/performance/limitations.<P>I used the Chris West crank side engine bracket rather than changing the cradle. Took forever to get it though. <P>I've only done the automatic trans swaps, not the manual trans. <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||TTiger||02-24-2001||02:43 PM||||Thanks terryk.  I looked last night into some of the archives.  There is some great information in some of those links.<BR>I am at this time planning to drop in an automatic transmission also, because I don't think I want to convert my auto Fiero to a 4 or 5 speed right now.  I will probably have more questions for you when I get the engine/ transmission.<P>Also, gabriel, I sent you an email for the PJ auto contacts.<P>Tony  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
